1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,928
This video is about deterministic finite automata, or D F As.

2
00:00:05,378 --> 00:00:21,378
A D F A is the target language of the regex compiler. We learn to read a D F A, run it, write it as a table, and design one from a small program. At the end we solve the execution questions from the question bank.

3
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:34,777
You specify tokens with regular expressions. But you need code: a lexer that walks the input and cuts it into tokens. So far, a person read the regexes and wrote that code.

4
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:47,153
We can do better. We generate the lexer from the regexes. This is a mini compiler. The source is a regular expression. The target is a program that recognizes it.

5
00:00:47,603 --> 00:00:56,093
The compiler has three steps. Regex to N F A. N F A to D F A. D F A to minimal D F A.

6
00:00:56,543 --> 00:01:09,599
The target must not be Java or C. We want a small language for recognizers, easy to execute anywhere. That language is the D F A. This video is about the D F A.

7
00:01:10,049 --> 00:01:21,249
A finite automaton is a machine with finitely many states. It reads an input string and accepts or rejects it. The plural of automaton is automata.

8
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:33,027
D F A means deterministic finite automaton. For each state and each input letter, there is exactly one next state. So a D F A follows one path.

9
00:01:33,477 --> 00:01:45,829
N F A means nondeterministic finite automaton. There may be several next states, or none. An N F A may also move on an epsilon edge, which reads no input.

10
00:01:46,279 --> 00:01:58,674
An N F A accepts if at least one path reads the whole input and ends in an accepting state. N F As are the topic of the next video. Today is about D F As.

11
00:01:59,124 --> 00:02:03,903
A D F A is a tiny programming language. It gets an input string.

12
00:02:04,353 --> 00:02:11,286
It reads one character at a time, and follows the transition for that character. It has no other memory.

13
00:02:11,736 --> 00:02:17,176
At the end of the string, it accepts if its state is accepting. Otherwise it rejects.

14
00:02:17,626 --> 00:02:33,925
D F As are easy to execute and easy to simulate. But they are long to write by hand, so we generate them. The goal: for any regular expression, build an automaton that accepts exactly the strings the regex matches.

15
00:02:34,375 --> 00:02:40,476
Here is the D F A from the slides. The states are the bubbles: q zero to q five.

16
00:02:40,926 --> 00:02:45,939
The initial state is the state with the arrow from nowhere. Here it is q zero.

17
00:02:46,389 --> 00:02:54,752
A transition is an arrow labelled with a letter of the alphabet. The alphabet is called sigma. Here sigma is a and b.

18
00:02:55,202 --> 00:02:59,959
An accepting state has a double circle. Here only q four accepts.

19
00:03:00,409 --> 00:03:09,796
Deterministic means: in every state, for every letter, exactly one arrow. Look at q two. It has one a arrow and one b arrow.

20
00:03:10,246 --> 00:03:17,990
State q five has a self loop on a and b. You can never leave it, and it does not accept. This is the error state.

21
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:23,496
Now run the machine. The input is a b a. Pause the video and try it.

22
00:03:23,946 --> 00:03:27,146
Start in q zero, before any letter is read.

23
00:03:27,596 --> 00:03:30,156
Read a. q zero goes to q one.

24
00:03:30,606 --> 00:03:33,273
Read b. q one goes to q two.

25
00:03:33,723 --> 00:03:42,939
Read the last a. q two goes to q five. The input is finished. q five does not accept, so the machine rejects a b a.

26
00:03:43,389 --> 00:03:45,287
Now the input a b b a.

27
00:03:45,737 --> 00:03:47,295
Start in q zero.

28
00:03:47,745 --> 00:03:49,451
Read a. Go to q one.

29
00:03:49,901 --> 00:03:51,885
Read b. Go to q two.

30
00:03:52,335 --> 00:03:54,490
Read b. Go to q three.

31
00:03:54,940 --> 00:04:02,215
Read a. Go to q four. The input is finished, and q four accepts. So the machine accepts a b b a.

32
00:04:02,665 --> 00:04:05,971
Take the input a b b. Start in q zero.

33
00:04:06,421 --> 00:04:08,213
Read a, go to q one.

34
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:10,583
Read b, go to q two.

35
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:18,436
Read b, go to q three. The input stops here. q three does not accept, so a b b is rejected.

36
00:04:18,886 --> 00:04:27,846
To accept a string, the machine scans the entire input, and the last state must be accepting. Nothing is decided before the end.

37
00:04:28,296 --> 00:04:43,165
Every string that is not a b b a ends in q five or in a state that does not accept. So the machine accepts exactly one string. Its language is the set with only a b b a, the regex a b b a.

38
00:04:43,615 --> 00:04:54,730
The error state is not part of the definition. q five is an ordinary state. Error is only our name for a state that you can never leave and never accept from.

39
00:04:55,180 --> 00:05:04,353
The course notes show the same idea on a smaller machine. Machine 1 accepts only the string a b. The alphabet is a and b.

40
00:05:04,803 --> 00:05:13,529
But some arrows are missing. State 1 has no b arrow. State 2 has no a arrow. State 3 has no arrows at all.

41
00:05:13,979 --> 00:05:23,344
There is an implicit error state. Call it X. Add an arrow to X for every letter that has no arrow. X loops to itself on every letter.

42
00:05:23,794 --> 00:05:27,613
Run a b. The path is 1, 2, 3, and 3 accepts.

43
00:05:28,063 --> 00:05:43,252
Run a. The path is 1, 2. The input ends in 2, so reject. Run a a: 1, 2, X, reject. Run a a b b: X keeps the machine in X. Reject. A missing arrow means: go to the error state.

44
00:05:43,702 --> 00:05:52,278
Machine 2 from the notes. State 1 is the start, and it accepts. a goes from 1 to 2. b goes from 2 back to 1.

45
00:05:52,728 --> 00:05:55,373
Run a b a. Start in state 1.

46
00:05:55,823 --> 00:05:57,850
Read a. Go to state 2.

47
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:04,124
Read b. Go back to state 1. State 1 accepts. But the input is not finished.

48
00:06:04,574 --> 00:06:13,001
Read the last a. Go to state 2. Now the input is finished, and state 2 does not accept. So a b a is rejected.

49
00:06:13,451 --> 00:06:29,131
The path passed through an accepting state, but that does not count. The question is only this: at the end of the input, is the machine in an accepting state? Machine 2 accepts the empty string, a b, a b a b, and so on.

50
00:06:29,581 --> 00:06:42,999
What kind of program is an automaton? Suppose you write it as a program. The input is a string. The program reads one character at a time. At the end of the file, it says accept or reject.

51
00:06:43,449 --> 00:06:49,081
A state of the automaton is the line number plus the values of all the variables.

52
00:06:49,531 --> 00:06:54,566
A transition is: read one character, run the line, and go to the next line.

53
00:06:55,016 --> 00:06:59,347
The end of the input is the decision: is the state double circled?

54
00:06:59,797 --> 00:07:11,381
This gives a design method. To get an automaton, write the program under these restrictions. Then compile it in your head. The automaton is the state space of the program.

55
00:07:11,831 --> 00:07:25,143
Which program features compile into an automaton? Loops compile: a transition can go backwards. Recursion does not compile: each call gets fresh local variables, and that is counting.

56
00:07:25,593 --> 00:07:32,419
If and switch on the next character compile. Dynamic memory and unbounded data structures do not.

57
00:07:32,869 --> 00:07:44,496
Finitely many variables of finite size compile. Nondeterminism does not compile into a real program, because real programs are deterministic.

58
00:07:44,946 --> 00:07:56,701
Count the states of a program like this. Five lines and two boolean variables give 5 times 2 times 2, which is 20 states. The number is large, but it is finite.

59
00:07:57,151 --> 00:08:04,276
An exercise from the slides. L is the set of strings over a and b with as many a letters as b letters.

60
00:08:04,726 --> 00:08:13,686
Write a program that recognizes L. Then check if every gadget you used compiles to an automaton. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:08:14,136 --> 00:08:23,523
Here is a program. A counter c starts at zero. Each a adds one. Each b subtracts one. At the end, accept if c is zero.

62
00:08:23,973 --> 00:08:28,410
The counter is the problem. Each counter value is a different state.

63
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:42,513
What is the width of the counter? 32 bits? 64 bits? The input has no bound, so no width is enough. We need arbitrary precision, and so memory on demand. That is not finite.

64
00:08:42,963 --> 00:08:54,569
This is not a proof. We tried one program and it failed. The pumping lemma gives the proof. But it is the method: write the program, check the gadgets, and count the states.

65
00:08:55,019 --> 00:09:00,245
The same machine, with three accepting states: q zero, q two and q four.

66
00:09:00,695 --> 00:09:04,322
Which strings does it accept? Pause the video and try it.

67
00:09:04,772 --> 00:09:14,009
q zero is the initial state, and it accepts. So the empty string is accepted. The machine reads nothing and ends in q zero.

68
00:09:14,459 --> 00:09:18,854
a b goes q zero, q one, q two. q two accepts.

69
00:09:19,304 --> 00:09:30,461
a b b a goes to q four, which accepts. Every other string ends in a state that does not accept. So L is the set: epsilon, a b, a b b a.

70
00:09:30,911 --> 00:09:40,789
Epsilon and lambda both mean the empty string. The notes use epsilon. Some books use lambda. Epsilon is not a letter of the alphabet.

71
00:09:41,239 --> 00:09:50,305
Change this automaton so that its language is infinite. What must you add? What must you also change? Pause the video and try it.

72
00:09:50,755 --> 00:10:00,761
A finite machine can accept infinitely many strings only with a cycle on an accepting path. For example, add a self loop on a at q one.

73
00:10:01,211 --> 00:10:08,699
But q one already has an a arrow, to q five. Now q one has two a arrows. That breaks determinism.

74
00:10:09,149 --> 00:10:16,103
So the old a arrow from q one to q five must go. Again there is one arrow per letter per state.

75
00:10:16,553 --> 00:10:33,321
The new language is epsilon, a b, a b b a, a a b, a a a b, and more. As a regex: epsilon, or a plus, b, then b a optional. A python check compared the machine and this regex on all strings up to length 9.

76
00:10:33,771 --> 00:10:39,339
Two more machines. What is the language of the first one? Pause the video and try it.

77
00:10:39,789 --> 00:10:52,845
q zero loops on a. Then one b goes to q one, which accepts. Any more letters go to q two, the error state. So the language is a star b: any number of a letters, then one b.

78
00:10:53,295 --> 00:11:02,469
The second machine has the alphabet with only the letter 1. Each 1 moves between q zero and q one. Only q zero accepts.

79
00:11:02,919 --> 00:11:13,564
So it accepts the strings of even length: epsilon, 1 1, 1 1 1 1, and so on. With one letter, the length is the only thing a string can encode.

80
00:11:14,014 --> 00:11:19,518
Now the length divisible by 3. Or by 7. How do you build these machines?

81
00:11:19,968 --> 00:11:28,160
Think as a programmer. Keep a counter n. Each letter adds one, modulo 3. At the end, accept if n is zero.

82
00:11:28,610 --> 00:11:39,511
The counter has three values: 0, 1 and 2. Each value is one state. Each 1 moves to the next remainder. Only remainder zero accepts.

83
00:11:39,961 --> 00:11:50,585
In general, a counter mod n needs n states, one per remainder. For 7, use 7 states in a ring. This counter is finite, so it compiles.

84
00:11:51,035 --> 00:12:01,083
A program cannot generate a picture. So we also write a D F A as a tuple of five parts: Q, sigma, delta, q zero, and F.

85
00:12:01,533 --> 00:12:05,331
Q is the set of states, here q zero to q five.

86
00:12:05,781 --> 00:12:14,442
Sigma is the alphabet, here a and b. Epsilon is not in sigma. Delta is the transition function: all the arrows.

87
00:12:14,892 --> 00:12:17,345
q zero is the initial state.

88
00:12:17,795 --> 00:12:24,046
F is the set of accepting states. Here F has only q four. F is a subset of Q.

89
00:12:24,496 --> 00:12:32,603
Delta maps a state and a letter to one state. So delta is a table, with one row per state and one column per letter.

90
00:12:33,053 --> 00:12:37,383
Row q zero. On a, go to q one. On b, go to q five.

91
00:12:37,833 --> 00:12:46,921
Rows q one and q two. On a, both go to q five. On b, q one goes to q two, and q two goes to q three.

92
00:12:47,371 --> 00:12:54,881
Row q three: a goes to q four, b goes to q five. Row q four: both letters go to q five.

93
00:12:55,331 --> 00:13:01,795
Row q five: both letters stay in q five. The table is the whole machine, written down.

94
00:13:02,245 --> 00:13:11,077
Determinism means that every cell of the table has exactly one state in it. No cell is empty, and no cell has two states.

95
00:13:11,527 --> 00:13:17,820
Now run a D F A formally. One step is delta of q and x. It gives the next state.

96
00:13:18,270 --> 00:13:37,769
For a whole string we use delta star. It applies delta letter by letter. Delta star of q and the empty string is q. Delta star of q and x w is delta star of delta of q and x, and w. So: take one step on the first letter, then continue with the rest.

97
00:13:38,219 --> 00:13:41,547
For example, delta star of q zero and a b.

98
00:13:41,997 --> 00:13:49,335
The first letter is a. Delta of q zero and a is q one. So we get delta star of q one and b.

99
00:13:49,785 --> 00:13:56,591
Delta of q one and b is q two. The rest is the empty string, so the answer is q two.

100
00:13:57,041 --> 00:14:10,779
A language over sigma is any set of strings. The language of M is every string w where delta star of q zero and w is in F. The rejected strings are the same with not in F.

101
00:14:11,229 --> 00:14:21,299
Some small machines over a and b. One state that does not accept, with a loop on both letters. Nothing is accepted. This is the empty language.

102
00:14:21,749 --> 00:14:28,554
The same machine, but the state accepts. Every string is accepted. This is sigma star, all strings.

103
00:14:29,004 --> 00:14:36,279
Two states. The start accepts, and any letter goes to an error state. Only the empty string is accepted.

104
00:14:36,729 --> 00:14:48,121
Strings that start with a b. a then b reaches q two, which accepts and loops on both letters. A wrong first or second letter goes to q three, the error state.

105
00:14:48,571 --> 00:14:53,179
Now the strings that do not start with a b. Pause the video and try it.

106
00:14:53,629 --> 00:14:59,047
Use the machine for starts with a b. Every string ends in exactly one state.

107
00:14:59,497 --> 00:15:06,217
Swap the accepting states. q zero, q one and q three now accept, and q two does not.

108
00:15:06,667 --> 00:15:20,598
Look at the short strings. The empty string, a, and b do not start with a b. They end in q zero, q one and q three. So those states must accept too, not only the error state q three.

109
00:15:21,048 --> 00:15:38,584
An exercise. The alphabet is 0 and 1. Accept the strings that contain 0 0 1. For example, 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 is accepted. 1 1 1 0 1 0 1 1 is rejected.

110
00:15:39,034 --> 00:15:53,242
Think like a programmer. What must you remember as you read the input? You cannot call String contains. Use boolean variables and small counters only. Then compile the program. Pause the video and try it.

111
00:15:53,692 --> 00:15:58,023
What must we remember? Only how many zeros in a row we have seen.

112
00:15:58,473 --> 00:16:08,201
The count is 0, 1 or 2. Each value is one state. Call them epsilon, 0, and 0 0, for the zeros seen so far.

113
00:16:08,651 --> 00:16:18,933
A 0 increments the counter. More than two zeros is the same as two: the counter saturates. So 0 0 loops to itself on 0.

114
00:16:19,383 --> 00:16:31,970
A 1 after fewer than two zeros resets the counter to the state epsilon. A 1 after two zeros completes 0 0 1. Go to the accepting state 0 0 1.

115
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:50,084
After that, the answer cannot change. The accepting state loops on 0 and 1, and eats the rest of the input. A python check ran the program and this D F A on all strings up to length 10. They agree. The program has exactly four states.

116
00:16:50,534 --> 00:16:56,806
Run the machine on 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0. Start in epsilon.

117
00:16:57,256 --> 00:16:59,645
Read 0. Go to state 0.

118
00:17:00,095 --> 00:17:04,575
Read 1. Only one zero was seen, so reset to epsilon.

119
00:17:05,025 --> 00:17:07,415
Read 0. Go to state 0.

120
00:17:07,865 --> 00:17:10,659
Read 0. Go to state 0 0.

121
00:17:11,109 --> 00:17:16,784
Read 0. Three zeros in a row is the same as two. Stay in 0 0.

122
00:17:17,234 --> 00:17:22,119
Read 1. That completes 0 0 1. Go to the accepting state.

123
00:17:22,569 --> 00:17:25,513
Read 0. Stay in 0 0 1.

124
00:17:25,963 --> 00:17:35,734
Read the last 0. Stay in 0 0 1. The input is finished, and the state accepts. So the string contains 0 0 1.

125
00:17:36,184 --> 00:17:51,693
The complement of a D F A keeps the states, the alphabet, the table and the start state. Only the accepting states change: the new accepting set is Q minus F. The new language is all strings minus the old language.

126
00:17:52,143 --> 00:18:02,981
So, does not contain 0 0 1: swap the accepting states. Epsilon, 0 and 0 0 now accept, and 0 0 1 does not.

127
00:18:03,431 --> 00:18:16,273
This works only because delta is total. Every string lands in exactly one state. That state is either in F or not in F. So think of the positive case, build it, then swap.

128
00:18:16,723 --> 00:18:24,574
Be careful with a drawing that leaves the error state implicit. Take machine 1 from the notes. It accepts only a b.

129
00:18:25,024 --> 00:18:31,125
Swap its accepting states without drawing X. Now 1 and 2 accept, and 3 does not.

130
00:18:31,575 --> 00:18:44,674
Run a a. From 2 there is no a arrow, so the machine goes to the implicit error state and rejects. But a a is not a b, so the complement must accept it. The swap gave a wrong machine.

131
00:18:45,124 --> 00:18:57,924
First draw the error state X with all its arrows. Then swap. Now X accepts, and a a is accepted. A python check confirms that this machine accepts every string except a b.

132
00:18:58,374 --> 00:19:16,379
Now the execution questions from the question bank. The alphabet is a, b and c. State 1 is the start. State 2 accepts. a or c goes from 1 to 2. b goes from 2 back to 1. Run the machine on a b c, on a b, and on a c b.

133
00:19:16,829 --> 00:19:18,472
Pause the video and try it.

134
00:19:18,922 --> 00:19:21,738
Input a b c. Start in state 1.

135
00:19:22,188 --> 00:19:24,236
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

136
00:19:24,686 --> 00:19:27,118
Read b. Go from 2 back to 1.

137
00:19:27,568 --> 00:19:34,352
Read c. Go from 1 to 2. The input is finished in state 2, which accepts. Output: accept.

138
00:19:34,802 --> 00:19:45,426
Input a b. a goes from 1 to 2, b goes from 2 to 1. The input ends in state 1. State 1 does not accept, so the output is reject.

139
00:19:45,876 --> 00:19:54,345
Input a c b. Here we need the error state. State 2 has no arrow for c. So we draw the implicit error state X.

140
00:19:54,795 --> 00:19:56,438
Start in state 1.

141
00:19:56,888 --> 00:19:58,936
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

142
00:19:59,386 --> 00:20:03,311
Read c. State 2 has no c arrow, so go to X.

143
00:20:03,761 --> 00:20:14,279
Read b. X stays in X. The input ends in X, so the output is reject. This matches the posted solution: 2 to X, then X to X.

144
00:20:14,729 --> 00:20:23,454
The question bank has a second machine. The b edge is replaced by an epsilon edge from 2 back to 1. Run it on a c.

145
00:20:23,904 --> 00:20:25,952
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

146
00:20:26,402 --> 00:20:34,829
Take the epsilon edge. Go from 2 back to 1. This reads no input. The solution writes a dash in the input column.

147
00:20:35,279 --> 00:20:41,743
Read c. Go from 1 to 2. The input is finished in state 2, so the output is accept.

148
00:20:42,193 --> 00:20:54,737
A machine with an epsilon edge is not a D F A. It is an N F A. This machine accepts every non empty string of a and c. The next video is about N F As.

149
00:20:55,187 --> 00:21:08,669
To summarize. A D F A is a tuple: states, alphabet, transition function, start state, and accepting states. The transition function is a table with exactly one state in every cell.

150
00:21:09,119 --> 00:21:16,671
To run a D F A, follow one arrow per letter. Accept only if the state at the end of the input is accepting.

151
00:21:17,121 --> 00:21:24,204
A missing arrow goes to the implicit error state. Draw the error state when you run a machine or complement it.

152
00:21:24,654 --> 00:21:36,771
To design a D F A, write a program with finite variables. Its state space is the D F A. A counter mod n compiles. An unbounded counter does not.

153
00:21:37,221 --> 00:21:49,381
To complement a D F A, keep the table and swap the accepting and non accepting states. The table must be total. Next video: N F As and regular languages.
